{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794133090758,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"id":"prt_1baaa61c6001oZ9qm1y8swuZx0","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1baaa504c0018cEmngQa2Ql7Ot","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"e9d3237c3f53ca929523bff518f390069d1cd83f","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":2477,"output":153,"reasoning":154,"cache":{"read":99584,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794133092123,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"id":"prt_1baaa671b001RfeLJpzybdHpLZ","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1baaa61ca001WzYzvDGC0A6O2I","type":"step-start","snapshot":"e9d3237c3f53ca929523bff518f390069d1cd83f"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794133093352,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"partID":"prt_1baaa6883001abfu5z676Steue","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1baaa61ca001WzYzvDGC0A6O2I","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a8c7a6f8af7b517","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '90,130p' \"REPO RULES.md\""},"output":"**Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\nof agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\nThose belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\nabsent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\nposture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n\n**The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\nroute stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\nIn; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\nthe mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\nadmits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n\n**The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\noverrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\nnaming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\nabove pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\nand what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\nruntimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\nasks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\na selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\nwhich of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\nit names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\nruntimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\nof agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\nThose belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\nabsent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\nposture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n\n**The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\nroute stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\nIn; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\nthe mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\nadmits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n\n**The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\noverrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\nnaming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\nabove pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\nand what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\nruntimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\nasks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\na selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\nwhich of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\nit names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\nruntimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '90,130p' \\\"REPO RULES.md\\\"\"}"},"time":{"start":1794133092483,"end":1794133093352}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794133093353,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"partID":"prt_1baaa6a77001XkDGj7WAn4dQ53","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1baaa61ca001WzYzvDGC0A6O2I","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-aba52a525c28196c","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '1,120p' .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs"},"output":"// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Chat Question Detection\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\n\nimport { chatSourceKey, isProcessQuestion } from './capture-core.mjs';\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst CHAT_CANDIDATES_PER_MESSAGE = 3;\nconst CHAT_CANDIDATES_PER_SWEEP = 20;\n\n// A paragraph ends at a blank line, and a run of blank lines is still one break.\nconst PARAGRAPH_BREAK = /(?:\\r?\\n[ \\t]*){2,}/;\n// The gate rules offer lettered choices; those letters are what the process\n// filter reads, so the detector hands them over the way the tool would.\nconst OPTION_LINE = /^\\s*(?:[-*]\\s+)?(?:\\*\\*)?[A-D]\\)/;\nconst OPTION_MARKER = /^\\s*(?:[-*]\\s+)?(?:\\*\\*)?/;\n\n// A reply is read as lowercased words, and a word is a run of these characters.\nconst WORD = /[a-z0-9']+/g;\n// Inline code can hold one side of a choice (\"`blue` or `amber`\"), so the type\n// check folds it into a neutral word rather than letting its own punctuation act\n// as a sentence break.\nconst INLINE_CODE = /\\x60[^\\x60\\n]*\\x60/g;\nconst CODE_PLACEHOLDER = 'code';\n// An ask sentence runs from the previous sentence break up to a question mark.\nconst ASK_SENTENCE = /(?:^|[.!:\\n])([^.!:\\n?]*)\\?/g;\nconst OR_WORD = /\\bor\\b/;\n// A numbered question asks several numbered things, not several lettered ones.\nconst NUMBERED_LINE = /^\\s*(?:\\*\\*)?\\d+[.)]/;\n// A reply can restate the numbering it answers without opening on a marker word.\nconst LIST_MARKER = /^\\s*\\d+\\s*[:.)]\\s*/;\nconst TOPIC_SHIFT_WINDOW = 5;\n\n// Words that carry no subject matter, so sharing one says nothing about intent.\nconst STOPWORDS = new Set([\n  'a', 'an', 'the', 'and', 'or', 'but', 'if', 'then', 'so', 'of', 'to', 'in',\n  'on', 'at', 'by', 'for', 'with', 'from', 'as', 'into', 'about', 'over', 'this',\n  'that', 'these', 'those', 'it', 'its', 'is', 'are', 'was', 'were', 'be',\n  'been', 'being', 'am', 'do', 'does', 'did', 'done', 'doing', 'have', 'has',\n  'had', 'having', 'will', 'would', 'shall', 'should', 'can', 'could', 'may',\n  'might', 'must', 'not', 'no', 'yes', 'yeah', 'yep', 'ok', 'okay', 'sure',\n  'please', 'i', 'me', 'my', 'we', 'our', 'us', 'you', 'your', 'he', 'she',\n  'they', 'them', 'their', 'what', 'which', 'who', 'whom', 'when', 'where',\n  'why', 'how', 'all', 'any', 'each', 'some', 'more', 'most', 'other', 'such',\n  'only', 'own', 'same', 'than', 'too', 'very', 'just', 'also', 'there', 'here',\n  'now', 'out', 'off', 'again', 'once', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'wasn', 'isn',\n  'aren', 'don', 'doesn', 'didn', 'won', 'wouldn', 'shouldn', 'couldn',\n  // Choice verbs sit on both sides of a choice without carrying it: \"use\n  // `Skilled`\" and \"use the darker one\" share the verb, not the answer.\n  'use', 'keep', 'make', 'pick', 'choose', 'take', 'want', 'need', 'like', 'try',\n  'get', 'go', 'let',\n]);\n\n// Verbs that ask for new work rather than approve the action a question names.\n// A trailing * marks a stem, so a misspelt \"Analyxe\" still reads as \"analyse\".\nconst IMPERATIVE_VERBS = [\n  'analy*', 'ask', 'audit', 'check', 'debug', 'do', 'explain', 'fix',\n  'investigat*', 'look', 'research', 'review', 'run', 'summari*',\n];\n\n// Politeness and sequence words a reply can open on before it gets to its verb.\nconst FILLERS = new Set([\n  'please', 'ok', 'okay', 'so', 'now', 'then', 'actually', 'first', 'just',\n  'also', 'and',\n]);\n\n// An imperative on one of these answers the question's own action: \"Do it\".\nconst ANAPHORS = new Set(['it', 'that', 'this', 'so', 'them']);\n\n// A reply that announces a different subject is not answering the question. A\n// bare \"first\" stays out: \"First, make it blue\" is a valid answer.\nconst SHIFT_PHRASES = [\n  'actually', 'instead', 'let\\'s', 'lets', 'let us', 'by the way', 'btw',\n  'never mind', 'nevermind', 'forget that', 'forget it', 'unrelated',\n];\n\nconst AUXILIARIES = new Set([\n  'is', 'are', 'was', 'were', 'do', 'does', 'did', 'should', 'shall', 'can',\n  'could', 'will', 'would', 'may', 'might', 'has', 'have', 'had',\n]);\n\nconst YES_NO = new Set([\n  'yes', 'yeah', 'yep', 'sure', 'ok', 'okay', 'no', 'nope', 'correct', 'right',\n]);\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 3. TEXT ANALYSIS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nfunction nonEmptyString(value) {\n  return typeof value === 'string' && value !== '';\n}\n\nfunction paragraphsOf(text) {\n  return text\n    .split(PARAGRAPH_BREAK)\n    .filter((paragraph) => paragraph.trim() !== '');\n}\n\n// The closing paragraph keeps its byte-exact candidate, while earlier paragraphs\n// are scanned because a reply can answer a question asked above.\nfunction lastParagraph(text) {\n  return paragraphsOf(text).at(-1) ?? '';\n}\n\n// Code and quotations can contain question marks without asking the operator anything.\nfunction prose(paragraph) {\n  return paragraph\n    .replace(/\\x60{3}[\\s\\S]*?\\x60{3}/g, '')\n    .replace(/\\x60[^\\x60\\n]*\\x60/g, '')\n    .replace(/\"[^\"\\n]*\"/g, '')\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Chat Question Detection\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\n\nimport { chatSourceKey, isProcessQuestion } from './capture-core.mjs';\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst CHAT_CANDIDATES_PER_MESSAGE = 3;\nconst CHAT_CANDIDATES_PER_SWEEP = 20;\n\n// A paragraph ends at a blank line, and a run of blank lines is still one break.\nconst PARAGRAPH_BREAK = /(?:\\r?\\n[ \\t]*){2,}/;\n// The gate rules offer lettered choices; those letters are what the process\n// filter reads, so the detector hands them over the way the tool would.\nconst OPTION_LINE = /^\\s*(?:[-*]\\s+)?(?:\\*\\*)?[A-D]\\)/;\nconst OPTION_MARKER = /^\\s*(?:[-*]\\s+)?(?:\\*\\*)?/;\n\n// A reply is read as lowercased words, and a word is a run of these characters.\nconst WORD = /[a-z0-9']+/g;\n// Inline code can hold one side of a choice (\"`blue` or `amber`\"), so the type\n// check folds it into a neutral word rather than letting its own punctuation act\n// as a sentence break.\nconst INLINE_CODE = /\\x60[^\\x60\\n]*\\x60/g;\nconst CODE_PLACEHOLDER = 'code';\n// An ask sentence runs from the previous sentence break up to a question mark.\nconst ASK_SENTENCE = /(?:^|[.!:\\n])([^.!:\\n?]*)\\?/g;\nconst OR_WORD = /\\bor\\b/;\n// A numbered question asks several numbered things, not several lettered ones.\nconst NUMBERED_LINE = /^\\s*(?:\\*\\*)?\\d+[.)]/;\n// A reply can restate the numbering it answers without opening on a marker word.\nconst LIST_MARKER = /^\\s*\\d+\\s*[:.)]\\s*/;\nconst TOPIC_SHIFT_WINDOW = 5;\n\n// Words that carry no subject matter, so sharing one says nothing about intent.\nconst STOPWORDS = new Set([\n  'a', 'an', 'the', 'and', 'or', 'but', 'if', 'then', 'so', 'of', 'to', 'in',\n  'on', 'at', 'by', 'for', 'with', 'from', 'as', 'into', 'about', 'over', 'this',\n  'that', 'these', 'those', 'it', 'its', 'is', 'are', 'was', 'were', 'be',\n  'been', 'being', 'am', 'do', 'does', 'did', 'done', 'doing', 'have', 'has',\n  'had', 'having', 'will', 'would', 'shall', 'should', 'can', 'could', 'may',\n  'might', 'must', 'not', 'no', 'yes', 'yeah', 'yep', 'ok', 'okay', 'sure',\n  'please', 'i', 'me', 'my', 'we', 'our', 'us', 'you', 'your', 'he', 'she',\n  'they', 'them', 'their', 'what', 'which', 'who', 'whom', 'when', 'where',\n  'why', 'how', 'all', 'any', 'each', 'some', 'more', 'most', 'other', 'such',\n  'only', 'own', 'same', 'than', 'too', 'very', 'just', 'also', 'there', 'here',\n  'now', 'out', 'off', 'again', 'once', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'wasn', 'isn',\n  'aren', 'don', 'doesn', 'didn', 'won', 'wouldn', 'shouldn', 'couldn',\n  // Choice verbs sit on both sides of a choice without carrying it: \"use\n  // `Skilled`\" and \"use the darker one\" share the verb, not the answer.\n  'use', 'keep', 'make', 'pick', 'choose', 'take', 'want', 'need', 'like', 'try',\n  'get', 'go', 'let',\n]);\n\n// Verbs that ask for new work rather than approve the action a question names.\n// A trailing * marks a stem, so a misspelt \"Analyxe\" still reads as \"analyse\".\nconst IMPERATIVE_VERBS = [\n  'analy*', 'ask', 'audit', 'check', 'debug', 'do', 'explain', 'fix',\n  'investigat*', 'look', 'research', 'review', 'run', 'summari*',\n];\n\n// Politeness and sequence words a reply can open on before it gets to its verb.\nconst FILLERS = new Set([\n  'please', 'ok', 'okay', 'so', 'now', 'then', 'actually', 'first', 'just',\n  'also', 'and',\n]);\n\n// An imperative on one of these answers the question's own action: \"Do it\".\nconst ANAPHORS = new Set(['it', 'that', 'this', 'so', 'them']);\n\n// A reply that announces a different subject is not answering the question. A\n// bare \"first\" stays out: \"First, make it blue\" is a valid answer.\nconst SHIFT_PHRASES = [\n  'actually', 'instead', 'let\\'s', 'lets', 'let us', 'by the way', 'btw',\n  'never mind', 'nevermind', 'forget that', 'forget it', 'unrelated',\n];\n\nconst AUXILIARIES = new Set([\n  'is', 'are', 'was', 'were', 'do', 'does', 'did', 'should', 'shall', 'can',\n  'could', 'will', 'would', 'may', 'might', 'has', 'have', 'had',\n]);\n\nconst YES_NO = new Set([\n  'yes', 'yeah', 'yep', 'sure', 'ok', 'okay', 'no', 'nope', 'correct', 'right',\n]);\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 3. TEXT ANALYSIS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nfunction nonEmptyString(value) {\n  return typeof value === 'string' && value !== '';\n}\n\nfunction paragraphsOf(text) {\n  return text\n    .split(PARAGRAPH_BREAK)\n    .filter((paragraph) => paragraph.trim() !== '');\n}\n\n// The closing paragraph keeps its byte-exact candidate, while earlier paragraphs\n// are scanned because a reply can answer a question asked above.\nfunction lastParagraph(text) {\n  return paragraphsOf(text).at(-1) ?? '';\n}\n\n// Code and quotations can contain question marks without asking the operator anything.\nfunction prose(paragraph) {\n  return paragraph\n    .replace(/\\x60{3}[\\s\\S]*?\\x60{3}/g, '')\n    .replace(/\\x60[^\\x60\\n]*\\x60/g, '')\n    .replace(/\"[^\"\\n]*\"/g, '')\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '1,120p' .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794133092983,"end":1794133093353}}}}
