{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794133520423,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"id":"prt_1bab0f027001eDC0u0wfZ1RwAs","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1bab0ebcd0012FAI2RUiMT4b2S","type":"step-start","snapshot":"217b6befe64bd4433545dd35d96183e3f98b568f"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794133542354,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"partID":"prt_1bab106760025xvnCrdU144vnL","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1bab0ebcd0012FAI2RUiMT4b2S","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-941cda3f06bfe218","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 3: Devin, Cursor, DSH and Hermes\n\n## Focus\n\nClose the runtime matrix. Devin, Cursor and DSH each have a decision-capture or hook adapter\nin this repository, so their ask-tool names can be proven locally. Hermes is named in the\nresearch topic but is expected to have been removed; that must be verified rather than\nassumed. This iteration also collapses the per-runtime answers into the blocking class and\nthe observe-only class.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n- Read the Devin tool inventory and the Devin decision-capture sweep.\n- Read the Devin permission-request policy, which is the one Devin event that emits a block.\n- Read the Cursor hook contract's event list, response envelope and exit semantics, then\n  searched the Cursor skill and `.cursor/` for any ask-tool name.\n- Read the DSH seam table, the adapter set, and the repo-guards README rows for\n  `decision-capture` and `completion-evidence`.\n- Read the Hermes deprecation changelog entry and the CLI hub's mode registry.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **Devin's ask tool is `ask_user_question`, and it is not available to subagents.** The tool inventory lists it under clarification with the explicit parenthetical \"not available to subagents\", and the decision-capture sweep keys on the same name. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md:212] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-devin.mjs:41]\n2. **Devin's only block-capable event in this repository is `PermissionRequest`, not `Stop`.** The permission policy emits `{decision: 'block', reason, hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision}`, while the Stop registrations run the advisory session-stop and completion-evidence adapters. A Devin turn-end guard would observe, not block, on today's contract. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/devin/permission-request-policy.mjs:102] [SOURCE: .devin/hooks.v1.json:1]\n3. **Cursor has no evidenced ask tool.** The Cursor skill's tool reference documents worktrees, the cloud worker, the plugin marketplace and MCP, but no question tool; `.skilled/hooks/decision-capture/` has no Cursor adapter; and no `ask_user_question`-style name appears in the Cursor references. Treat Cursor as a runtime without a native ask surface until a tool name is proven. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/cursor-tools.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/README.md:1]\n4. **Cursor's turn-end events are post-hoc.** `afterAgentResponse` and `stop` are listed as documented agent events, and the response envelope carries `permission: allow | deny | ask`, but the envelope is a gate for the action a hook intercepts; the repository's Cursor completion adapter returns no decision at all and is advisory. Cursor turn-end blocking is therefore unproven, and the honest classification is observe-only. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/hook-contract.md:83] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/cursor/completion-evidence-response.mjs:1]\n5. **DSH's ask tool is `ask_user_question`, and headless runs have no question tool.** The repo-guards adapter table says so in the decision-capture row: \"runs, headless has no question tool\". Since fan-out lineages are headless, a DSH worker cannot satisfy an ask-the-operator obligation. [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:94] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-dsh.mjs:33]\n6. **DSH is the only non-Claude runtime with a genuine turn-end block seam.** `agent/turn-stopping` is documented as \"Force another step before the turn ends\", and the repository's own completion-evidence adapter is attached there — but with the error policy \"Log and continue\", so it observes. The seam exists; the policy is what would change. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-deepseek-harness/references/guards-and-hooks.md:53] [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:84]\n7. **Hermes is no longer a runtime in this repository.** The v4.0.2.0 changelog states that `cli-hermes`, the `.hermes/` folder and the `hermes` launcher function are gone, `.hermes/` is absent from the checkout, and the CLI hub's mode registry holds seven modes with no `cli-hermes` entry. Any rule text or hook wiring that still names Hermes is stale. [SOURCE: .skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.0.md:47] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/mode-registry.json:1]\n8. **The runtime matrix resolves into three classes.** Blocking turn-end seams exist on Claude Code (`Stop`, `decision: \"block\"`) and DSH (`agent/turn-stopping`, \"force another step\"). Codex, Cursor and Devin have turn-end events with no documented block contract in this repository, so they are observe-only in practice. OpenCode and Pi have no turn-end seam at all. Hermes is out of scope. [INFERENCE: from findings 1-7 above]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- **Devin `Stop` as a blocking guard.** The repository uses Devin's blocking decision on\n  `PermissionRequest`; no Stop block contract exists here.\n- **Cursor as a runtime with a native ask tool.** No tool name, no sweep, no adapter. Naming\n  one would be exactly the invented tool the handoff rule forbids.\n- **Keeping Hermes in the matrix.** Verified removed, so the honest answer is \"out of scope\",\n  not \"unknown\".\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Cursor turn-end blocking: the hook contract documents the envelope but not which post-hoc\n  events honour it, and no live adapter relies on it. Recorded as unproven.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: whether Cursor has an ask tool in a newer release than this repository\n  records. Recorded as \"no evidenced tool\", not \"no tool exists\".\n- Contradictory evidence: the research topic names Hermes as in scope; the changelog and the\n  mode registry say it was removed. The repository evidence wins.\n- Missing dependencies: no upstream Cursor or Devin hook documentation reachable locally.\n- Partial success: none; the matrix is complete.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-devin.mjs\n- .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/devin/permission-request-policy.mjs\n- .devin/hooks.v1.json\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/cursor-tools.md\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/hook-contract.md\n- .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/cursor/completion-evidence-response.mjs\n- .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-deepseek-harness/references/guards-and-hooks.md\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-dsh.mjs\n- .skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.0.md\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/mode-registry.json\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.94\n- Questions addressed: Q1, Q2\n- Questions answered: Q1 in full — every runtime in scope now has a proven tool name or a\n  proven absence, with its availability gate.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: reading each runtime's own adapter table gave a tool name and a gate in\n  the same row, which is why the matrix closed in one iteration.\n- What did not work and why: Cursor and Devin have no local hook-block contract, so their\n  turn-end classification is an absence-of-evidence finding rather than a proven negative.\n- What I would do differently: check the changelog before the runtime list next time; Hermes\n  cost a search that a version note would have answered immediately.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nIteration 4: the detector. Establish the heuristic (option list plus a question near the end,\nno ask-tool call in the turn), measure its false-positive surface on ordinary numbered steps,\nsummaries and code blocks, and assemble a fixture set from the repository's existing tests.\n","path":"specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 3: Devin, Cursor, DSH and Hermes\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nClose the runtime matrix. Devin, Cursor and DSH each have a decision-capture or hook adapter\\nin this repository, so their ask-tool names can be proven locally. Hermes is named in the\\nresearch topic but is expected to have been removed; that must be verified rather than\\nassumed. This iteration also collapses the per-runtime answers into the blocking class and\\nthe observe-only class.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n- Read the Devin tool inventory and the Devin decision-capture sweep.\\n- Read the Devin permission-request policy, which is the one Devin event that emits a block.\\n- Read the Cursor hook contract's event list, response envelope and exit semantics, then\\n  searched the Cursor skill and `.cursor/` for any ask-tool name.\\n- Read the DSH seam table, the adapter set, and the repo-guards README rows for\\n  `decision-capture` and `completion-evidence`.\\n- Read the Hermes deprecation changelog entry and the CLI hub's mode registry.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. **Devin's ask tool is `ask_user_question`, and it is not available to subagents.** The tool inventory lists it under clarification with the explicit parenthetical \\\"not available to subagents\\\", and the decision-capture sweep keys on the same name. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md:212] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-devin.mjs:41]\\n2. **Devin's only block-capable event in this repository is `PermissionRequest`, not `Stop`.** The permission policy emits `{decision: 'block', reason, hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision}`, while the Stop registrations run the advisory session-stop and completion-evidence adapters. A Devin turn-end guard would observe, not block, on today's contract. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/devin/permission-request-policy.mjs:102] [SOURCE: .devin/hooks.v1.json:1]\\n3. **Cursor has no evidenced ask tool.** The Cursor skill's tool reference documents worktrees, the cloud worker, the plugin marketplace and MCP, but no question tool; `.skilled/hooks/decision-capture/` has no Cursor adapter; and no `ask_user_question`-style name appears in the Cursor references. Treat Cursor as a runtime without a native ask surface until a tool name is proven. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/cursor-tools.md:1] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/README.md:1]\\n4. **Cursor's turn-end events are post-hoc.** `afterAgentResponse` and `stop` are listed as documented agent events, and the response envelope carries `permission: allow | deny | ask`, but the envelope is a gate for the action a hook intercepts; the repository's Cursor completion adapter returns no decision at all and is advisory. Cursor turn-end blocking is therefore unproven, and the honest classification is observe-only. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/hook-contract.md:83] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/cursor/completion-evidence-response.mjs:1]\\n5. **DSH's ask tool is `ask_user_question`, and headless runs have no question tool.** The repo-guards adapter table says so in the decision-capture row: \\\"runs, headless has no question tool\\\". Since fan-out lineages are headless, a DSH worker cannot satisfy an ask-the-operator obligation. [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:94] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-dsh.mjs:33]\\n6. **DSH is the only non-Claude runtime with a genuine turn-end block seam.** `agent/turn-stopping` is documented as \\\"Force another step before the turn ends\\\", and the repository's own completion-evidence adapter is attached there — but with the error policy \\\"Log and continue\\\", so it observes. The seam exists; the policy is what would change. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-deepseek-harness/references/guards-and-hooks.md:53] [SOURCE: .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md:84]\\n7. **Hermes is no longer a runtime in this repository.** The v4.0.2.0 changelog states that `cli-hermes`, the `.hermes/` folder and the `hermes` launcher function are gone, `.hermes/` is absent from the checkout, and the CLI hub's mode registry holds seven modes with no `cli-hermes` entry. Any rule text or hook wiring that still names Hermes is stale. [SOURCE: .skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.0.md:47] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/mode-registry.json:1]\\n8. **The runtime matrix resolves into three classes.** Blocking turn-end seams exist on Claude Code (`Stop`, `decision: \\\"block\\\"`) and DSH (`agent/turn-stopping`, \\\"force another step\\\"). Codex, Cursor and Devin have turn-end events with no documented block contract in this repository, so they are observe-only in practice. OpenCode and Pi have no turn-end seam at all. Hermes is out of scope. [INFERENCE: from findings 1-7 above]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- **Devin `Stop` as a blocking guard.** The repository uses Devin's blocking decision on\\n  `PermissionRequest`; no Stop block contract exists here.\\n- **Cursor as a runtime with a native ask tool.** No tool name, no sweep, no adapter. Naming\\n  one would be exactly the invented tool the handoff rule forbids.\\n- **Keeping Hermes in the matrix.** Verified removed, so the honest answer is \\\"out of scope\\\",\\n  not \\\"unknown\\\".\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Cursor turn-end blocking: the hook contract documents the envelope but not which post-hoc\\n  events honour it, and no live adapter relies on it. Recorded as unproven.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: whether Cursor has an ask tool in a newer release than this repository\\n  records. Recorded as \\\"no evidenced tool\\\", not \\\"no tool exists\\\".\\n- Contradictory evidence: the research topic names Hermes as in scope; the changelog and the\\n  mode registry say it was removed. The repository evidence wins.\\n- Missing dependencies: no upstream Cursor or Devin hook documentation reachable locally.\\n- Partial success: none; the matrix is complete.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-devin.mjs\\n- .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/devin/permission-request-policy.mjs\\n- .devin/hooks.v1.json\\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/cursor-tools.md\\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-cursor/references/hook-contract.md\\n- .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/cursor/completion-evidence-response.mjs\\n- .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md\\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-deepseek-harness/references/guards-and-hooks.md\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-dsh.mjs\\n- .skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.0.md\\n- .skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/mode-registry.json\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.94\\n- Questions addressed: Q1, Q2\\n- Questions answered: Q1 in full — every runtime in scope now has a proven tool name or a\\n  proven absence, with its availability gate.\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: reading each runtime's own adapter table gave a tool name and a gate in\\n  the same row, which is why the matrix closed in one iteration.\\n- What did not work and why: Cursor and Devin have no local hook-block contract, so their\\n  turn-end classification is an absence-of-evidence finding rather than a proven negative.\\n- What I would do differently: check the changelog before the runtime list next time; Hermes\\n  cost a search that a version note would have answered immediately.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nIteration 4: the detector. Establish the heuristic (option list plus a question near the end,\\nno ask-tool call in the turn), measure its false-positive surface on ordinary numbered steps,\\nsummaries and code blocks, and assemble a fixture set from the repository's existing tests.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794133526134,"end":1794133542354}}}}
