{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794133617297,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"partID":"prt_1bab2690c0010voV4pHYJowHDD","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1bab24313001W3GS7CMePyz1ig","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-92dbbd34d0691009","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/fixtures/ | head -30; echo \"=== TRANSCRIPT TOOL ===\"; sed -n '35,50p' .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-transcript.mjs"},"output":"claude-chat-answers-extended.jsonl\nclaude-chat-answers-extended.labels.json\nclaude-chat-questions.jsonl\nclaude-chat-questions.labels.json\ncodex-chat-rollout.jsonl\ncodex-request-user-input.json\ndevin-chat-session.json\ndevin-transcript.json\nopencode-chat-session.json\nopencode-question-parts.json\nopencode-session-message.json\npi-ask-user-question.json\npi-chat-session.jsonl\n=== TRANSCRIPT TOOL ===\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst RUNTIME = 'claude';\nconst QUESTION_TOOL = 'AskUserQuestion';\nconst TRANSCRIPT_EXT = '.jsonl';\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 3. TRANSCRIPT LINES\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// A killed process leaves a torn final line, and a sidechain is a sub-agent's own\n// conversation: neither belongs to the operator's turn sequence. Both are skipped\n// rather than failing the sweep, because a partial backfill still beats none.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"claude-chat-answers-extended.jsonl\nclaude-chat-answers-extended.labels.json\nclaude-chat-questions.jsonl\nclaude-chat-questions.labels.json\ncodex-chat-rollout.jsonl\ncodex-request-user-input.json\ndevin-chat-session.json\ndevin-transcript.json\nopencode-chat-session.json\nopencode-question-parts.json\nopencode-session-message.json\npi-ask-user-question.json\npi-chat-session.jsonl\n=== TRANSCRIPT TOOL ===\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst RUNTIME = 'claude';\nconst QUESTION_TOOL = 'AskUserQuestion';\nconst TRANSCRIPT_EXT = '.jsonl';\n\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 3. TRANSCRIPT LINES\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// A killed process leaves a torn final line, and a sidechain is a sub-agent's own\n// conversation: neither belongs to the operator's turn sequence. Both are skipped\n// rather than failing the sweep, because a partial backfill still beats none.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"ls .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/fixtures/ | head -30; echo \\\"=== TRANSCRIPT TOOL ===\\\"; sed -n '35,50p' .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-transcript.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794133616908,"end":1794133617297}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794133618181,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"id":"prt_1bab26e05001yJ580GeeKbqa3v","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1bab24313001W3GS7CMePyz1ig","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"dd29d9fdd6b0d67f7fa0cbf1d01e270d5ba37114","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":365,"output":93,"reasoning":948,"cache":{"read":233088,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794133632743,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"id":"prt_1bab2a6e7001yXDr24qbTln6Dv","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1bab26e0b001dinUbNAi1ZtTi1","type":"step-start","snapshot":"dd29d9fdd6b0d67f7fa0cbf1d01e270d5ba37114"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794133633217,"sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","part":{"partID":"prt_1bab2a6e9001Htgurm9Km4joJM","sessionID":"ses_e45574eb5fferLWklFUKCqoqXo","messageID":"msg_1bab26e0b001dinUbNAi1ZtTi1","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d815486183be9fa","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 4: Detector heuristic, false-positive surface and fixtures\n\n## Focus\n\nDefine the detector that decides a turn ended in a prose option menu without an ask-tool\ncall. The repository already ships a question detector for decision capture; the question is\nwhether it can be reused, what it misses, and how often it would fire on ordinary numbered\nsteps, summaries and code blocks.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n- Read the chat-question detector's constants, prose stripper, option matcher and candidate\n  selection.\n- Read the process-question filter, which suppresses scope, permission, gate and shipping\n  questions from capture.\n- Read the detector's 13 tests and listed the per-runtime fixtures already in the tree.\n- Read the Claude transcript sweep's tool constant, which proves the \"did an ask tool run\"\n  half of the rule is already implemented per runtime.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **A question detector already exists and is close to the needed shape.** `chat-questions.mjs` reads the closing paragraph, requires a `?` in prose, collects lettered options from that paragraph or an immediately following options-only paragraph, and skips process questions. The ask-surface guard can reuse it instead of inventing a second parser. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:120] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:277]\n2. **Its option grammar is too narrow for enforcement.** `OPTION_LINE` accepts only `A)` through `D)`, optionally bulleted or bolded. A numbered menu (`1)`, `2)`), an `A.` style, or an `Option 1:` label is invisible to it, so a guard built on the constant alone would miss real prose menus. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:28]\n3. **The prose stripper is the anti-code-block defense.** Before testing for a question mark, it removes fenced code, inline code, straight and curly quotes, and blockquote, table and heading lines. A `?` inside a code sample or a quoted transcript therefore cannot open a candidate. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:118]\n4. **Ordinary numbered steps are a low false-positive risk.** The gate is the option list, not the number, and step lists use `1.` at the margin while the option matcher requires an `A)`-shaped marker. A reply can be a numbered plan and still contain no candidate. [INFERENCE: from the option regex at chat-questions.mjs:28 and the numbered-line regex at chat-questions.mjs:49]\n5. **Summaries are a low risk only while a question mark is required.** A closing \"Anything else?\" has no option list, so it fails the option gate. A summary that both asks a question and prints four `A)`-shaped lines would fire, which is the intended behavior but also the residual false positive. [INFERENCE: from the candidate rule at chat-questions.mjs:277]\n6. **Two real false-positive vectors remain: options-only code blocks and blockquoted menus.** `isOptionsOnly` and `optionLabelsOf` read raw lines, while only the question test is prose-stripped. A paragraph that is entirely a code block of `A)` lines, or a quoted menu inside a blockquote, can satisfy the option half. The guard must strip fences and quotes before option extraction, not only before the question test. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:133]\n7. **The \"no ask-tool call in the turn\" half is already implemented per runtime.** Claude's transcript sweep keys on `AskUserQuestion`, Codex on `request_user_input`, OpenCode on `question`, Pi on `ask_user_question` or `question`, Devin and DSH on `ask_user_question`. A guard can reuse those readers to prove the negative. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-transcript.mjs:41] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-codex.mjs:38] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-opencode.mjs:46] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-pi.mjs:35]\n8. **Claude's Stop payload cannot prove the negative by itself.** `last_assistant_message` carries the text, but whether an ask tool ran in the turn lives in the transcript, so the Claude guard needs the payload for the text and `transcript_path` for the tool-use scan. [SOURCE: https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks]\n9. **A fixture base already exists and needs negatives, not positives.** The tree holds positive ask fixtures for Claude, Codex, OpenCode, Pi and Devin, plus 13 detector tests; what it lacks is the negative set a guard must not fire on: numbered steps, a summary question with no options, a fenced code menu, a blockquoted menu, a single option, and a question already asked through the tool. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/fixtures/claude-chat-questions.jsonl] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.test.mjs:10]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- **Reusing `isProcessQuestion` as the guard's filter.** It suppresses scope, permission, gate\n  and shipping questions from *capture*, which is the opposite polarity: those are exactly the\n  decisions the ask surface exists to carry. Reusing it would silence the guard on Gate 3.\n- **A pure `last_assistant_message` check with no tool scan.** It cannot distinguish a turn\n  that asked through the tool from one that printed a menu.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- None. The detector's limits are documented in its own constants and tests.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: whether a numbered menu should count as a prose menu. Decided yes; the\n  grammar must be widened or the guard has a known blind spot.\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\n- Missing dependencies: none.\n- Partial success: the fixture set is proposed, not created; creation belongs to implementation.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.test.mjs\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/capture-core.mjs\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-transcript.mjs\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-codex.mjs\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-opencode.mjs\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-pi.mjs\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/fixtures/\n- https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.9\n- Questions addressed: Q3\n- Questions answered: Q3 in full, including the false-positive classes and a fixture set.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: reading the detector's constants first made the false-positive analysis\n  concrete, because each risk maps to one regex and one function.\n- What did not work and why: nothing failed. The detector's option grammar is the one real gap,\n  and it was visible in a single line.\n- What I would do differently: list the fixtures before the tests next time; the positive-only\n  fixture set explains why the detector was never tuned against menus it must reject.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nIteration 5: wiring and rule text. Map the guard onto `hook-registry.json`,\n`sync-hook-registrations.cjs` and the `.skilled/hooks/<name>/<runtime>/` layout, decide the\nsync-versus-async and loop-guard details, define the escape hatch, and draft the exact\namendments for `communication-handoff.md` §4/§5 and `AGENTS.md` Gate 3 inside the ask-surface\ncarve-out.\n","path":"specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-004.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-004.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-004.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 4: Detector heuristic, false-positive surface and fixtures\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nDefine the detector that decides a turn ended in a prose option menu without an ask-tool\\ncall. The repository already ships a question detector for decision capture; the question is\\nwhether it can be reused, what it misses, and how often it would fire on ordinary numbered\\nsteps, summaries and code blocks.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n- Read the chat-question detector's constants, prose stripper, option matcher and candidate\\n  selection.\\n- Read the process-question filter, which suppresses scope, permission, gate and shipping\\n  questions from capture.\\n- Read the detector's 13 tests and listed the per-runtime fixtures already in the tree.\\n- Read the Claude transcript sweep's tool constant, which proves the \\\"did an ask tool run\\\"\\n  half of the rule is already implemented per runtime.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. **A question detector already exists and is close to the needed shape.** `chat-questions.mjs` reads the closing paragraph, requires a `?` in prose, collects lettered options from that paragraph or an immediately following options-only paragraph, and skips process questions. The ask-surface guard can reuse it instead of inventing a second parser. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:120] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:277]\\n2. **Its option grammar is too narrow for enforcement.** `OPTION_LINE` accepts only `A)` through `D)`, optionally bulleted or bolded. A numbered menu (`1)`, `2)`), an `A.` style, or an `Option 1:` label is invisible to it, so a guard built on the constant alone would miss real prose menus. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:28]\\n3. **The prose stripper is the anti-code-block defense.** Before testing for a question mark, it removes fenced code, inline code, straight and curly quotes, and blockquote, table and heading lines. A `?` inside a code sample or a quoted transcript therefore cannot open a candidate. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:118]\\n4. **Ordinary numbered steps are a low false-positive risk.** The gate is the option list, not the number, and step lists use `1.` at the margin while the option matcher requires an `A)`-shaped marker. A reply can be a numbered plan and still contain no candidate. [INFERENCE: from the option regex at chat-questions.mjs:28 and the numbered-line regex at chat-questions.mjs:49]\\n5. **Summaries are a low risk only while a question mark is required.** A closing \\\"Anything else?\\\" has no option list, so it fails the option gate. A summary that both asks a question and prints four `A)`-shaped lines would fire, which is the intended behavior but also the residual false positive. [INFERENCE: from the candidate rule at chat-questions.mjs:277]\\n6. **Two real false-positive vectors remain: options-only code blocks and blockquoted menus.** `isOptionsOnly` and `optionLabelsOf` read raw lines, while only the question test is prose-stripped. A paragraph that is entirely a code block of `A)` lines, or a quoted menu inside a blockquote, can satisfy the option half. The guard must strip fences and quotes before option extraction, not only before the question test. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs:133]\\n7. **The \\\"no ask-tool call in the turn\\\" half is already implemented per runtime.** Claude's transcript sweep keys on `AskUserQuestion`, Codex on `request_user_input`, OpenCode on `question`, Pi on `ask_user_question` or `question`, Devin and DSH on `ask_user_question`. A guard can reuse those readers to prove the negative. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-transcript.mjs:41] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-codex.mjs:38] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-opencode.mjs:46] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-pi.mjs:35]\\n8. **Claude's Stop payload cannot prove the negative by itself.** `last_assistant_message` carries the text, but whether an ask tool ran in the turn lives in the transcript, so the Claude guard needs the payload for the text and `transcript_path` for the tool-use scan. [SOURCE: https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks]\\n9. **A fixture base already exists and needs negatives, not positives.** The tree holds positive ask fixtures for Claude, Codex, OpenCode, Pi and Devin, plus 13 detector tests; what it lacks is the negative set a guard must not fire on: numbered steps, a summary question with no options, a fenced code menu, a blockquoted menu, a single option, and a question already asked through the tool. [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/fixtures/claude-chat-questions.jsonl] [SOURCE: .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.test.mjs:10]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- **Reusing `isProcessQuestion` as the guard's filter.** It suppresses scope, permission, gate\\n  and shipping questions from *capture*, which is the opposite polarity: those are exactly the\\n  decisions the ask surface exists to carry. Reusing it would silence the guard on Gate 3.\\n- **A pure `last_assistant_message` check with no tool scan.** It cannot distinguish a turn\\n  that asked through the tool from one that printed a menu.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- None. The detector's limits are documented in its own constants and tests.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: whether a numbered menu should count as a prose menu. Decided yes; the\\n  grammar must be widened or the guard has a known blind spot.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\\n- Missing dependencies: none.\\n- Partial success: the fixture set is proposed, not created; creation belongs to implementation.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.mjs\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/chat-questions.test.mjs\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/lib/capture-core.mjs\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-transcript.mjs\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-codex.mjs\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-opencode.mjs\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/sweep-pi.mjs\\n- .skilled/hooks/decision-capture/bin/fixtures/\\n- https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.9\\n- Questions addressed: Q3\\n- Questions answered: Q3 in full, including the false-positive classes and a fixture set.\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: reading the detector's constants first made the false-positive analysis\\n  concrete, because each risk maps to one regex and one function.\\n- What did not work and why: nothing failed. The detector's option grammar is the one real gap,\\n  and it was visible in a single line.\\n- What I would do differently: list the fixtures before the tests next time; the positive-only\\n  fixture set explains why the detector was never tuned against menus it must reject.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nIteration 5: wiring and rule text. Map the guard onto `hook-registry.json`,\\n`sync-hook-registrations.cjs` and the `.skilled/hooks/<name>/<runtime>/` layout, decide the\\nsync-versus-async and loop-guard details, define the escape hatch, and draft the exact\\namendments for `communication-handoff.md` §4/§5 and `AGENTS.md` Gate 3 inside the ask-surface\\ncarve-out.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/027-ask-surface-enforcement/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-004.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794133632745,"end":1794133633217}}}}
